; numerology/name — choosing the same way twice, for nothing.
;
; A gematria is a deterministic function from text to a number: letters become
; figures, the figures are summed, the sum is reduced. It is the oldest such
; reading there is and mechanically it is an ordinary hash — which is exactly
; what makes it useful here, and exactly the limit of what it can claim.
;
; What it gives a program: a free, reproducible choice. Rebis already has one
; free deterministic judge, the symbol mediator that scores by holonomy. This
; is a second, on a different measure and in the same slot. Where a run must
; pick and nothing about the pick needs to be RIGHT — a tie to break, an
; exploration to shard, a selection that must survive a re-run byte for byte —
; a model call buys nothing and a reading costs nothing.
;
; What it does not give: any claim that the number means something. The
; collection says this about Jung and it is truer here — these are composition
; tools, not diagnostic tests. A route chosen by a name is reproducible, not
; correct, and every macro below is written so nothing else could be inferred.
;
; The gate this family is held to is therefore not accuracy but DETERMINISM:
; same text, same figure, same branch, forever. That is testable, and it is the
; whole of what is promised.
(
  (# std/measure)

  ; (name-value text) — the figure a text reduces to.
  ;
  ;   text   any text. Interpolated, never fired.
  ;
  ; Letters are counted, the sum is taken to a single figure, and case and
  ; punctuation change nothing — so the same subject written two ways reads the
  ; same, which is the only property anything here depends on.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (name-value the-retry-queue)
  ;
  ; Cost: nothing.
  (~ name-value (text) '(std-root (std-weight ,text)))

  ; (name-shares a b) — do two texts reduce alike?
  ;
  ;   a b   texts. Interpolated, never fired.
  ;
  ; Answers 0 or 1, which is what `%` reads — so a reduction can steer an
  ; ordinary program with no control flow added anywhere.
  ;
  ; Cost: nothing.
  (~ name-shares (a b)
    '(std-equal (name-value ,a) (name-value ,b)))

  ; (name-splits text left right) — send a subject one way or the other, the
  ; same way every time.
  ;
  ;   text    the subject deciding. Interpolated, never fired.
  ;   left    complete expression, taken by an even figure.
  ;   right   complete expression, taken by an odd figure.
  ;
  ; A deterministic two-way shard. Which side a subject lands on is arbitrary;
  ; that it lands on the same side every run is the point, and is the only
  ; thing that should ever be relied on.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (name-splits the-subject (read-it-closely) (read-it-broadly))
  ;
  ; Cost: exactly one branch. The reduction is free.
  (~ name-splits (text left right)
    '(% |(/ 2 (name-value ,text))| ,right ,left))

  ; (name-breaks-tie a b text) — pick between two equals, reproducibly.
  ;
  ;   a b    complete expressions, taken by an even and an odd figure.
  ;   text   what decides. Interpolated, never fired.
  ;
  ; The honest use, and the one worth having. When two options have been judged
  ; and neither won, something must still choose — and a model asked again
  ; gives a different answer than it gave the first time, which makes the run
  ; unrepeatable for no gain. This chooses badly but identically, which is
  ; strictly better than choosing badly and differently.
  ;
  ; Cost: exactly one branch.
  (~ name-breaks-tie (a b text) '(name-splits ,text ,a ,b))

  ; (name-numbered text work) — hand a worker the subject and its figure.
  ;
  ;   text   the subject. Interpolated into the framing, never fired.
  ;   work   complete expression. Every prompt inside sees both.
  ;
  ; For the case where the figure belongs in the prompt rather than in the
  ; control flow — a framing, not a decision. `+` reaches every prompt in the
  ; body, and the reduction it carries cost nothing to compute.
  ;
  ; Cost: exactly what work costs.
  (~ name-numbered (text work)
    '(+ ($ ,text " reduces to " (name-value ,text) ".") ,work)))
